\chapter{Validation Protocol}

\section{Overview}

The AAAHSC--9.4 validation protocol establishes a sovereign-grade
verification framework ensuring numerical stability, physical
consistency, cryptographic integrity, and distributed scalability.  
Validation is performed across six independent stages:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Baseline Numerical Stability
    \item AMR Refinement Integrity
    \item Semiclassical Backreaction Stability
    \item Secure Telemetry Ingestion
    \item Multi-Node Scalability
    \item Cross-Regime Homeostasis
\end{enumerate}

Each stage is designed to stress-test a different subsystem of the
architecture, ensuring that AAAHSC--9.4 performs reliably under
strong-field, semiclassical, and observationally driven conditions.

\section{Stage I: Baseline Numerical Stability}

Baseline stability is assessed by evolving a vacuum Schwarzschild
spacetime using the full BSSN $3+1$ formulation across the AMR hierarchy.

Metrics include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Hamiltonian constraint norm $\|\mathcal{H}\|_2$,
    \item Momentum constraint norm $\|\mathcal{M}\|_2$,
    \item Gauge stability under $1+\log$ and Gamma-driver conditions,
    \item Absence of secular drift in curvature invariants.
\end{itemize}

The simulation must maintain:
\begin{equation}
    \|\mathcal{H}\|_2, \|\mathcal{M}\|_2 \le 1.21 \times 10^{-13}.
\end{equation}

Constraint norms are monitored continuously, and any deviation triggers
the homeostasis controller.

\section{Stage II: AMR Refinement Integrity}

AMR refinement is validated by inducing controlled curvature gradients and
verifying:

\begin{itemize}
    \item correct refinement triggering at $\theta \le 10^{-3}$,
    \item proper prolongation and restriction across levels,
    \item flux synchronization without numerical artifacts,
    \item GPU-resident execution of all boundary operations.
\end{itemize}

Cross-level consistency is measured using:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta Q_{\ell,\ell+1} =
    \left\| Q^{\ell} - \mathcal{R}(Q^{\ell+1}) \right\|_2.
\end{equation}

Refinement must remain stable even under strong-field curvature spikes.

\section{Stage III: Semiclassical Backreaction Stability}

The semiclassical backreaction module is validated by evolving a
near-horizon region with Hawking flux enabled.

Stability criteria include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item bounded negative-energy sink magnitude,
    \item monotonic horizon mass-loss rate,
    \item absence of curvature blow-up,
    \item consistent integration into $T_{\mu\nu}$.
\end{itemize}

Mass-loss rate is compared against:
\begin{equation}
    \dot{M}_{\text{sim}} \approx \dot{M}_{\text{theory}}.
\end{equation}

The horizon tracker must maintain accurate MOTS detection throughout the
evaporative phase.

\section{Stage IV: Secure Telemetry Ingestion}

Telemetry ingestion is validated using encrypted Gaia DR3 and LIGO O4
streams.

Validation metrics include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item correctness of lattice-based PQC decryption,
    \item preservation of data provenance,
    \item absence of injection-induced constraint drift,
    \item correct mapping of telemetry into boundary conditions.
\end{itemize}

Telemetry packets undergo:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item signature verification,
    \item timestamp validation,
    \item provenance confirmation,
    \item numerical consistency checks.
\end{enumerate}

Packets failing verification are discarded without affecting the
simulation.

\section{Stage V: Multi-Node Scalability}

The Kubernetes/MPI topology is validated by deploying AAAHSC--9.4 across
heterogeneous GPU clusters.

Metrics include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item MPI domain decomposition consistency,
    \item non-blocking inter-node communication latency,
    \item AMR block placement stability under Kubernetes scheduling,
    \item linear or near-linear scaling with node count.
\end{itemize}

Elastic scaling is tested by dynamically increasing refinement levels and
verifying that Kubernetes launches additional pods and MPI reassigns
ranks without interrupting evolution.

\section{Stage VI: Cross-Regime Homeostasis}

The homeostasis controller is validated by evolving the system through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item weak-field regimes,
    \item strong-field regimes,
    \item near-horizon regimes,
    \item semiclassical evaporative regimes.
\end{itemize}

Homeostasis is confirmed when:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d}{dt} \|\mathcal{H}\|_2 \approx 0, \qquad
    \frac{d}{dt} \|\mathcal{M}\|_2 \approx 0.
\end{equation}

The controller must maintain constraint norms within threshold across all
regimes.

